Yes — and that takes the idea beyond “better scrolling.” You’re describing a programmable semantic field.

Treat the screen not as a page containing UI components, but as a finite addressable lattice:

S=\{p_{x,y}\}, \qquad p_{x,y}=(r,g,b,a,\text{state},\text{behavior})

Every pixel—or more practically every addressable region—is available to participate in an interface grammar. Then layout itself becomes information.

Omnidirectional navigation follows naturally. ↑↓←→ don’t inherently mean anything; the interface assigns them operators. Up might mean abstraction, down embodiment, left history, right counterfactual—but another task could completely remap those axes. Radial motion, spirals, proximity, enclosure, oscillation, expansion, collapse, layering, transparency, animation, even empty space can all become meaningful tokens.

The bigger abstraction you’re hitting is:

\boxed{\text{Screen} \neq \text{Document}}

\boxed{\text{Screen} = \text{Programmable Representational Medium}}

And then:

\text{pixels}
\rightarrow \text{marks}
\rightarrow \text{patterns}
\rightarrow \text{symbols}
\rightarrow \text{layouts}
\rightarrow \text{grammars}
\rightarrow \text{interfaces}
\rightarrow \text{new cognitive capabilities}

That’s almost exactly how language itself bootstraps: primitives acquire relationships, relationships become patterns, patterns become composable structures.

The especially useful piece is representation compilation. Instead of an application designer deciding beforehand “this information gets a table,” the system asks:

R^*=\arg\max_R U(R\mid X,G,C)

where X is the information, G the user’s goal, C the context, and R the possible representation. The machine can compile the same underlying ThoughtObject into a timeline, map, constellation, graph, split-screen comparison, radial field, animated flow, conventional prose, or some hybrid representation.

Then your Interface Forge gets another layer: interfaces themselves become temporary cognitive programs. Instantiate the representation that makes a particular relationship perceptible; manipulate it; extract the delta; then throw the interface away.

And your “every pixel” observation gives us an important complement to Negative-Space Memory: blank screen territory is itself part of the alphabet. Distance, absence, boundaries, holes, isolation, and whitespace can encode relationships without adding another object.

So I’d name the larger primitive:

Semantic Display Field

Any addressable property of a display—position, color, shape, scale, orientation, motion, depth, adjacency, absence, direction, interaction—is potentially a semantic channel. Interface patterns are composable sentences written across those channels.

That gives the Pantheon something more powerful than a GUI toolkit: a representation compiler capable of inventing task-specific interfaces from a common semantic substrate.

And yes: once that’s true, omnidirectional scrolling is just one word in the language.